  §34.5-§34.7 printed in full (short sections; the pointer at L9580 is tested on the text):
    L9623 ### 34.5 The corridor
    L9625 Requiring the observed subshell to have least ν gives one linear inequality per
    L9626 rival, and their intersection is an interval:
    L9628 > **L(Z) < a < U(Z)**,  **L, U = Δn(√p_g + √p_r)/(p_g − p_r)**
    L9630 **All 106 non-empty. Nineteen distinct surds across the whole table. No
    L9631 measurement enters — only Pauli's capacity and the node count.**
    L9633 *For every ns/(n−1)d competition the crossing is (√(n−1) + √(n−4))/3, giving
    L9634 0.5773503, 1.0000000, 1.2168450, 1.3938270 at n = 4 to 7.*
    L9636 ### 34.6 The walk
    L9638 **`a` is carried between elements and resets eighteen times.** Every reset is a
    L9639 **subshell opening** (8), an **aufbau exception** (6), or the **return from one**
    L9640 (4) — Tc after Mo, Tb after Gd, Bk after Cm, Hg after Au. **It never resets
    L9641 mid-subshell**, which is why each subshell fills at constant `a`.
    L9643 *But eighteen is the cost of walking Z in order, not the cost of the table.*
    L9644 Read as an arrangement of intervals, the 106 corridors are pierced by **three**
    L9645 values of `a`, and three are forced: boron, lanthanum and lawrencium have
    L9646 pairwise disjoint corridors, so no two of them can share one. The largest
    L9647 disjoint set and the smallest piercing set agree at three, which certifies both
    L9648 (register 1580).
    L9650 **And the state is necessary — to the form.** With no carried value every step
    L9651 admits more than one self-consistent subshell: two to six across the table, and
    L9652 one never. *The observed one is always among them and never uniquely
    L9653 determined.* **That much is geometry.** In the plane where each subshell is the
    L9654 point (√r, n), ν takes whichever point a line of slope `a` reaches first, and a
    L9655 point set with two distinct abscissae always offers more than one such point, at
    L9656 every neutral atom there is. **What is not geometry is that the observed subshell
    L9657 is among them at all 106 steps.** That is the corridor, and it is what survives
    L9658 the form's demotion (§34.4; registers 1445, 1460, 1463).
    L9660 **And the carried value is what the ordering costs.** In that same plane the
    L9661 Madelung number is **M = 2n − p − 1** (register 1460) — with x = √p, the parabola
    L9662 **M = 2y − x² − 1**, where ν sweeps straight lines. No single slope follows a
    L9663 parabola, which is why no fixed `a` reaches it and why a re-set one does: **the
    L9664 number carried between elements is the price of reading a quadratic order off a
    L9665 linear form**, and the Madelung pick lies inside the corridor at every one of the
    L9666 106 steps.
    L9668 ### 34.7 The entry point
    L9670 Where `a` sits in its corridor is fixed by the single-state observation — the
    L9671 ionisation energy — and the position is
    L9673 > **t(ℓ) → √( ℓ(ℓ+1) / 2 )**
    L9675 **Measured 1.028 at p across four subshells and 1.785 at d across two: the ratio
    L9676 is √3 to 0.19%.** *ℓ(ℓ+1) is the centrifugal term, the only place ℓ enters the
    L9677 radial Schrödinger equation.*
    L9679 **And it is a limit approached along n**, not a constant: the p row of Λ_t reads
    L9680 1.120, 1.049, 1.022, 1.002 at n = 3, 4, 5, 6 — **6p sits 0.2% above the
    L9681 centrifugal value.**

  the two out-of-unit sites of 108 (from test 5), read:
